      "id": "9di9whr"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "t640d0w",
      "content": "Control and coordination enable organisms to respond to stimuli and maintain internal balance (homeostasis). In humans, coordination occurs through the Nervous System and Endocrine System. In plants, coordination is regulated by plant hormones."
    },
    {
      "id": "mq9bhje",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "26llgnt",
      "src": "/books/Precis/RRB GS/images/image_26llgnt.jpeg"
    },
    {
      "id": "obbk122",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. Need for Control & Coordination"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ut0n86g",
      "content": "• Respond to external stimuli (light, heat, touch)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "8bpukdy",
      "content": "• Maintain internal balance."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "wja1apa",
      "content": "• Coordinate voluntary and involuntary actions."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "97m5nl1",
      "content": "• Regulate growth and development."
    },
    {
      "id": "baes963",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. Nervous System – Overview"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "efd1uyt",
      "content": "The nervous system controls body activities through electrical impulses. The functional unit is the neuron, which transmits nerve impulses."
    },
    {
      "id": "u4vkyyy",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. Structure of Neuron"
    },
